<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>



<section xml:id="sec-gas5" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Ideal gas law</title>

	<p>
		Avogadro's law and the other gas laws can all be combined together.
		Pressure and volume are inversely proportional to one another (Boyle's Law):
		<me>P\times V=c</me>
		Likewise, volume is directly proportional to both temperature (Charles' Law) and number of moles of a gas (Avogadro's Law)
		<me>\frac{V}{T}=c</me> and <me>frac{V}{n}=c</me>
		If we combine all of this information, we get the equation: 
		<me>\frac{P\cdot V}{n\cdot T}=c</me>
		 where c
		is a constant.
		In chemistry, we substitute the symbol R for this constant to give:
		<me>\frac{P\cdot V}{n\cdot T}=R</me>
		The equation shown above is called the Ideal Gas Law and is typically shown in the
		rearranged form:
	

	<me>
		PV = nRT
	</me>
	</p><p>
		In the Ideal Gas Law, the constant R is known as the gas constant.
		There are several different values for R which are all actually
		equivalent...they have different values because
	
		they have different units of pressure or volume.
		The value of R that uses SI units of Pa for pressure and L for volume is <m>8314 \frac{Pa\cdot L}{mol\cdot K}</m>.  Other values of R are shown in the table below:
		</p>
<p>
		<table>
			<title>Various Values for the Gas Constant R</title>
			<tabular>
				
			<row header="yes" bottom="major">
				<cell halign="center">Value</cell>
				<cell halign="center">Units</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell halign="center">8314</cell>
					<cell halign="center"><m>\frac{Pa\cdot L}{mol\cdot K}</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell halign="center">0.082057</cell>
					<cell halign="center"><m>\frac{L\cdot atm}{mol\cdot K}</m></cell>
				</row>	
				<row>
					<cell halign="center">0.0831446</cell>
					<cell halign="center"><m>\frac{L\cdot bar}{mol\cdot K}</m></cell>
				</row>		
<row>
					<cell halign="center">1.21</cell>
					<cell halign="center"><m>\frac{L\cdot psi}{mol \cdot K}</m></cell>
				</row>
				</tabular>
				</table>
</p>		

	<p>
		When should we make use of the Ideal Gas Law vs. the other laws.  The other laws that we learned about earlier in this chapter are useful for predicting the effect of
		changing the conditions of a gas: either the pressure, volume, temperature, or
		moles of gas.
		The Ideal Gas Law, on the other hand, allows us to calculate one unknown property of a gas if
		we're given the other 3 properties.
		For instance, if we know the pressure, volume, and temperature of a sample of
		gas, we can calculate the number of moles of gas.
	</p>

	
	<exercises>
		<title>Ideal Gas Law</title>

		<exercise>
			<statement>
				<p>
					If a sample of gas contains 0.0300 moles of gas and has a pressure of 2300
					Pa at a temperature of 273K, what would the volume of the gas be?
				</p>
			</statement>

			<answer>
				<p>
					30.
					L
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Calculate the temperature of a gas if 3.0 moles of gas have a volume of 43L
					and a pressure of 2.3 atm.
					(1 atm=101325 Pa)
				</p>
			</statement>

			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>4.0 x 10^2 K</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise>
			<statement>
				<p>
					What is the pressure of a gas in units of MPa if 2.3 moles of gas are
					placed into a 6.7L container at <m>103 ^oC</m>?
				</p>
			</statement>

			<answer>
				<p>
					1.1 MPa
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>
	</exercises>
</section>